From b3fbab006cbd2cfca4cdc229dc14540a4cd86b17 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: WilbertACDX <115293547+WilbertACDX@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 16:39:47 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Android/GLES port: window-surface display path, colour
 order, VRAM dirty uploads

Patches the vendored core for multiemu's Android frontend (arm64, GLES
3.2 on an Adreno 610). Grouped by what they fix:

GLES portability
- GPU3D_OpenGL.cpp: GL_UNSIGNED_SHORT_1_5_5_5_REV doesn't exist in GLES
  and silently no-ops the palette upload, so unpack ABGR1555 on the CPU
  and upload as GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE instead.
- GPU3D_OpenGL_shaders.h: usampler2D has no default precision in GLES;
  Adreno tolerated the omission, ANGLE correctly rejected it.
- Assorted MSVC-portability fixes (DSi_DSP, DSi_AES, ARCodeFile,
  ARDatabaseDAT, DSi_NWifi, GPU2D_Soft, GPU3D, NonStupidBitfield, SPU)
  so the same tree builds for the desktop RenderDoc harness used to
  debug all of the above.

Display path
- GPU_OpenGL: BlitOutputToScreen blits the compositor's output straight
  to the bound window surface, replacing a
  glReadPixels -> JNI int[] -> Bitmap -> Canvas round trip per frame.
  ReadOutputScreenAsync keeps that CPU path alive as a fallback, but via
  a PBO so it no longer stalls the CPU behind the GPU.
- GPU3D.h/GPU3D_OpenGL.h: the virtuals the frontend needs to reach both.
- GPU_OpenGL_shaders.h: the compositor's final swizzle is .rgb, not
  upstream's .bgr. Upstream only ever samples that texture; we blit it to
  an RGBA8 window, where .bgr lands red and blue crossed (a blue sky
  renders orange).

Performance
- GPU3D_OpenGL.cpp: do what upstream's own
  "SUCKY!!! TODO: detect when VRAM blocks are modified!" asks for. It
  re-uploaded all 4x128K of texture VRAM plus 6 CPU-unpacked palette
  blocks every 3D frame -- ~700K of texture traffic per frame, measured
  as the largest single block of driver time in the emulation thread
  (23.6% of it, simpleperf on device). The tracking already exists and
  the software renderer already uses it (VRAMDirty + DeriveState, 512
  byte granularity), so use it here too and skip untouched blocks.

Measured on a CMA-LX3 (Adreno 610) walking around outdoors in Pokemon
SoulSilver: 21fps -> 56-57fps, 36ms -> 15.7ms per frame.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
